@using System.Globalization
@using BlazorDLR.Shared.Diagnostics
@using BlazorDLR.Shared.Services
@using BlazorDLR.Shared.State
@using DLR.Core.Tracks
@inject IMapInterop Map
@inject MapSourceState MapSources
@implements IAsyncDisposable

@*
	Composes the base map (§4.5 v0.24: MapLibre GL JS over OSM, the same module on every
	host) with the shared Skia overlay that draws every marker, track and rider pin.

	Two layers stacked in a positioned container:

	  1. `dlr-map-base` - a plain <div> the JS module attaches to. Handles pan / zoom /
		 rotate / tiles / attribution, and emits a MapViewport when the view moves.
	  2. `SkiaMapOverlay` - a SkiaSharp canvas positioned absolutely over the base, with
		 `pointer-events: none` so gestures pass through. Repaints on every viewport
		 event.

	"A map that cannot get a token shows a stated error, not a blank grey rectangle"
	(§4.5) - the try/catch below is where that decision is made. v0.24 removed the token,
	but not the rule: an unreachable CDN or tile server fails here the same way.

	The overlay gets an ErrorBoundary of its own, and the reason is a failure this project
	has already had. The overlay used to be an <SKCanvasView> from SkiaSharp.Views.Blazor,
	which initialises through `[JSImport]` - WebAssembly-only interop - so on a MAUI
	BlazorWebView, where the runtime is Mono, its first render threw. An unhandled throw in
	a child's OnAfterRenderAsync does not just lose that child: it takes down the Blazor
	renderer for the whole app. On device that read as a base map that still panned, because
	it is pure JS in the WebView, while every button on every page stopped responding.

	v0.24 fixed the cause - the overlay now rasterises off-screen and hands the frame to an
	<img> (see SkiaMapOverlay) - and the boundary stays because the blast radius is the whole
	application rather than one component, and because the overlay is the one child here that
	touches a native rasteriser on three different runtimes.

	It is not idle insurance: it has fired in the field. A repaint that outlived its component
	drew into a released Skia surface and the null that came back reached here as "Map markers
	unavailable", with a wrapper's message for evidence and nothing in the log. The overlay now
	stops its own paints at teardown and contains its own failures (see SkiaMapOverlay), so
	anything that still reaches this boundary is something nobody has seen before - which is
	exactly why it is a LoggingErrorBoundary and not a plain one.
*@

<div class="dlr-map-container @(OnMapClicked.HasDelegate && ShowPickingCursor ? "picking" : "")">
	<div @ref="_host" class="dlr-map-base"></div>

	@if (_viewport is { CanvasWidthPx: > 0 } vp)
	{
		@*
			Without the boundary the failure is unrecoverable for the whole renderer, so this is
			not the usual "wrap it just in case".

			LoggingErrorBoundary, not the stock one: a boundary is the last place an exception is
			ever seen - recovering is the point of one - so a boundary that only renders a message
			loses it for good. This writes the type, the stack and every wrapped exception to the
			diagnostic log first, which is the only way to find out what failed on a phone in a
			mount (Settings → Log).
		*@
		<LoggingErrorBoundary @ref="_overlayBoundary" Context="the map overlay">
			<ChildContent>
				<SkiaMapOverlay Viewport="vp" Markers="Markers" Routes="Routes" Circles="Circles" Boxes="Boxes" />
			</ChildContent>
			<ErrorContent Context="overlayError">
				@*
					Deliberately not silent. Without the overlay there are no rider pins, no
					markers and no route on the map - on a live group ride that is the whole
					point of the screen, so a rider has to be told rather than left reading an
					empty map as "nobody is moving" (§5.3, §16.3).
				*@
				<div class="dlr-map-banner dlr-map-overlay-error" role="alert">
					<strong>Map markers unavailable</strong>
					<p>The base map is working, but pins, tracks and traveller positions cannot be drawn on this device.</p>
					@*
						Summarise, not Message. A wrapper's own message is "One or more errors
						occurred." followed by a parenthesised quote of what it holds, which names
						nothing a rider or a developer can act on; this unwraps to the exception
						that actually failed and states its type. The full detail - stacks and all
						- is in the log, and the line below says so, because a banner on a map is
						not where a stack trace belongs.
					*@
					<p class="dlr-map-overlay-error-detail">@DiagnosticLog.Summarise(overlayError)</p>
					<p class="dlr-map-overlay-error-detail">The full details are in Settings → Log.</p>
				</div>
			</ErrorContent>
		</LoggingErrorBoundary>
	}

	@*
		The map is working and drawing empty ground - see IMapInterop.CoverageChanged. It goes on
		the map rather than under it because the empty map is the evidence.
	*@
	@if (ShowNoTilesWarning)
	{
		<div class="dlr-map-banner dlr-map-no-tiles" role="status">
			<strong>No offline tiles in this area at zoom @TileZoomText</strong>
			<p>The downloaded pack does not reach here. Pan back, or choose another map in Settings → Maps.</p>
		</div>
	}
</div>

@if (_error is not null)
{
	<div class="dlr-map-error" role="alert">
		<strong>Map unavailable</strong>
		<p>@_error</p>
	</div>
}
else if (_tileError is not null)
{
	@*
		The map attached but cannot draw. Its own class of failure: a tile source that will not
		answer, a style whose glyphs are missing, an offline archive the WebView will not fetch -
		all of which leave a working map object rendering nothing at all.

		Shown rather than swallowed because the alternative is a blank rectangle, and a rider
		cannot tell that from a broken app. The wording says what is missing rather than what
		threw, and the detail is there because the first person to hit one is usually the person
		who can fix it.
	*@
	<div class="dlr-map-error" role="alert">
		<strong>Map tiles unavailable</strong>
		<p>The map is running but could not load its tiles. Settings → Maps offers another source.</p>
		<p class="dlr-map-error-detail">@_tileError</p>
	</div>
}

@code {
	/// <summary>Where the map opens.</summary>
	[Parameter, EditorRequired] public MapCamera Camera { get; set; } = default!;

	/// <summary>
	/// Ground the map must show all of, framed at the closest zoom that still holds it.
	/// <para>
	/// Set by the pages that open on a route somebody already rode - the track detail and the
	/// track editor - where the interesting view is the whole line and nothing else. It is applied
	/// over <see cref="Camera"/> rather than instead of it: the camera is where the map
	/// <em>opens</em>, which the base map needs before it has a canvas to measure, and the fit is
	/// the correction once it has one. Give both, and give a camera on the middle of the box so
	/// the two never disagree about which ground is being talked about.
	/// </para>
	/// <para>
	/// <para>
	/// The live ride passes one too, and passes it exactly once: the box round the ride's planned
	/// routes, the first time a rider opens that ride. Its view is not a fixed extent - it follows
	/// a rider, and the rider pans it - so it sets the box for the opening frame and then leaves
	/// the value alone forever. That works because a box already fitted is ignored (see
	/// <see cref="ApplyBoundsAsync"/>), so the parameter is inert from the second render onward.
	/// </para>
	/// <para>
	/// Null on the pickers, where the rider is choosing a point and being re-framed under their
	/// hand would take the map off what they were aiming at.
	/// </para>
	/// </summary>
	[Parameter] public TrackBounds? Bounds { get; set; }

	/// <summary>
	/// CSS pixels between <see cref="Bounds"/> and the edge of the map. Clamped by the module to
	/// what the canvas can spare, so a small map is framed tight rather than refusing to frame.
	/// </summary>
	[Parameter] public double BoundsPaddingPx { get; set; } = 32;

	/// <summary>
	/// How far in <see cref="Bounds"/> may zoom. Only binds on a box smaller than the map - a
	/// track round a car park, or one whose points all landed on the same fix, which would
	/// otherwise be framed at the deepest zoom the tiles have.
	/// </summary>
	[Parameter] public double BoundsMaxZoomLevel { get; set; } = 16;

	/// <summary>Whether the base map draws its own current-position marker.</summary>
	[Parameter] public bool ShowUserLocation { get; set; }

	/// <summary>
	/// Whether the rider may turn the map off north. On by default; a compass appears top-left
	/// once they do, and tapping it turns the map back.
	/// <para>
	/// Set false on the two screens where a tap <em>places</em> something - the private-area
	/// picker (§10.1) and the marker composer (§16.1). There the map is a coordinate entry
	/// field, and a rider who has rotated it is aiming at a north-up mental image that is no
	/// longer on screen. Tilting is refused everywhere regardless; see <see cref="MapOptions"/>.
	/// </para>
	/// </summary>
	[Parameter] public bool AllowRotation { get; set; } = true;

	/// <summary>
	/// The routes drawn under the pins (§15.5). A list, because a group ride may have several
	/// planned routes (§5.4) - a track detail passes a one-element one.
	/// </summary>
	[Parameter] public IReadOnlyList<RouteOverlay>? Routes { get; set; }

	/// <summary>Markers to draw (§16). Keyed on id for idempotent upsert on the Skia side.</summary>
	[Parameter] public IReadOnlyDictionary<Guid, MapMarker>? Markers { get; set; }

	/// <summary>
	/// Ground circles drawn under the routes and pins. Set by the private-area picker on the
	/// profile screen (§10.1, §18.6), which is a radius on the ground and has to look like one.
	/// </summary>
	[Parameter] public IReadOnlyList<MapCircle>? Circles { get; set; }

	/// <summary>
	/// Lat / lon rectangles drawn under everything else. Set by the map-pack picker on the settings
	/// screen (§4.2), where each box is the ground one downloadable region covers.
	/// </summary>
	[Parameter] public IReadOnlyList<MapBox>? Boxes { get; set; }

	/// <summary>
	/// Which tiles to draw, overriding the rider's own setting for this map alone.
	/// <para>
	/// Null - and so the device's choice, followed as it changes - everywhere but the map-pack
	/// picker (§4.2). That map is a picture of the whole world, and the two sources it may find
	/// stored cannot be one: an offline pack holds a single region and would leave the rest of the
	/// world blank, and a tile server the rider is part-way through configuring may not answer at
	/// all. So it opens on what it can use and falls back to OpenStreetMap, which is a decision
	/// about that screen rather than about the rider's map, and must not be written to the device.
	/// </para>
	/// <para>
	/// A map given one stops following <c>MapSourceState</c>: the caller is holding the answer, and
	/// a device change arriving underneath would take it back.
	/// </para>
	/// </summary>
	[Parameter] public MapSource? Source { get; set; }

	/// <summary>
	/// Fired with the base map's first complaint - a tile source it cannot reach, a style it cannot
	/// parse (see <see cref="IMapInterop.ErrorOccurred"/>). Once per map, like the message the
	/// component shows itself, because one unreachable source raises an error per tile.
	/// <para>
	/// Set by the map-pack picker, which answers it by re-pointing <see cref="Source"/> at OSM: on
	/// that screen a map that draws nothing is not a message to read, it is the whole interface
	/// missing.
	/// </para>
	/// </summary>
	[Parameter] public EventCallback<string> OnTileError { get; set; }

	/// <summary>Fired once the base map has attached and reported a viewport.</summary>
	[Parameter] public EventCallback OnReady { get; set; }

	/// <summary>
	/// Fired when the user taps a point on the base map, in decimal degrees. Unset on the
	/// live ride map - nothing there is placed by pointing - and set by the marker composer,
	/// which is the one screen where a tap means "here" (§16.1).
	/// </summary>
	[Parameter] public EventCallback<MapClick> OnMapClicked { get; set; }

	/// <summary>
	/// Whether a wired-up <see cref="OnMapClicked"/> also turns the pointer into a crosshair.
	/// <para>
	/// On by default, because the composer was the only caller and there a tap means "put it
	/// here" (§16.1). The live ride page turns it off: a tap there <em>interrogates</em> what is
	/// already on the map rather than placing anything, and a crosshair over a whole live ride
	/// invites people to think they are about to drop a pin.
	/// </para>
	/// </summary>
	[Parameter] public bool ShowPickingCursor { get; set; } = true;

	/// <summary>
	/// Fired whenever the base map pans, zooms or rotates. Lets a caller carry the view the
	/// user is actually looking at somewhere else - the live ride page hands it to the marker
	/// composer so the picker opens on the same ground (§16.1).
	/// </summary>
	[Parameter] public EventCallback<MapViewport> OnViewportReported { get; set; }

	/// <summary>
	/// Fired when the rider moved the map themselves - a drag, a twist, or the compass - as
	/// opposed to the map having been moved for them by <see cref="SetCameraAsync"/>.
	/// <para>
	/// The live ride page is the caller: it drives the camera from two automatic modes, and each
	/// has to let go the instant a hand takes the map (§5.3). <see cref="OnViewportReported"/>
	/// cannot serve - it reports where the map is, never who put it there - so this is a separate
	/// event rather than a flag on that one. See <see cref="MapGesture"/>.
	/// </para>
	/// </summary>
	[Parameter] public EventCallback<MapGesture> OnGesture { get; set; }

	private ElementReference _host;
	private LoggingErrorBoundary? _overlayBoundary;
	private bool _initialised;
	private MapCamera? _appliedCamera;

	/// <summary>
	/// The box the map has already been framed on, or null if it never has been. The same "have I
	/// sent this yet" marker <see cref="_appliedCamera"/> is, and it earns its place for the same
	/// reason: a page that re-renders for any other cause - a status line, a rating arriving -
	/// would otherwise re-fit the map underneath a rider who had panned away from the route.
	/// </summary>
	private TrackBounds? _appliedBounds;

	/// <summary>
	/// The tile source the base map is currently drawing, or null before it has attached - the
	/// same "is the map there yet" marker <see cref="_appliedCamera"/> is, and used the same way.
	/// <para>
	/// It is what stops a restyle the map does not need. <c>MapSourceState.LoadAsync</c> raises
	/// <c>Changed</c> when the device read resolves, and that lands while this component is still
	/// inside its own first render - so without the comparison every map ever opened would follow
	/// its init with a <c>setStyle</c> to the source it had just opened with, and flash its tiles
	/// doing it.
	/// </para>
	/// </summary>
	private MapSource? _appliedSource;
	private string? _error;

	/// <summary>
	/// The first thing the base map said it could not do, or null while it is drawing fine.
	/// <para>
	/// <strong>First only.</strong> One unreachable source raises an error per tile, so keeping the
	/// latest would flicker a different message several times a second, and keeping all of them
	/// would bury the one that mattered under its own repetitions.
	/// </para>
	/// </summary>
	private string? _tileError;

	/// <summary>
	/// How many errors the base map has raised over this component's life. Shown nowhere - it is
	/// the log's, and it is what separates one bad glyph from a source that cannot be read at all.
	/// </summary>
	private int _mapErrors;

	/// <summary>How often a continuing failure repeats itself in the log. See <see cref="OnMapError"/>.</summary>
	private const int MapErrorLogEvery = 50;

	/// <summary>
	/// When <see cref="RetryOfflineSourceAsync"/> last re-resolved, on the monotonic tick count, or
	/// zero for never.
	/// <para>
	/// A cooldown rather than the once-per-component flag this used to be. The thing it recovers
	/// from - the loopback listener going quiet while the phone suspends the app - can happen more
	/// than once on a ride left running overnight, and a single spent retry left the rider with a
	/// dead map for the rest of the run.
	/// </para>
	/// </summary>
	private long _retriedSourceTicks;

	/// <summary>
	/// The shortest gap between two re-resolves. Long enough that a pack which is genuinely
	/// unreadable restyles the map twice a minute rather than once per failed tile.
	/// </summary>
	private const long SourceRetryCooldownMs = 30_000;

	private MapViewport? _viewport;

	/// <summary>
	/// What the base map last said about tile coverage, or null before it has said anything - not
	/// the same as "covered", which is why this is nullable rather than a bool.
	/// </summary>
	private MapCoverage? _coverage;

	protected override void OnInitialized()
	{
		Map.ViewportChanged += OnViewportChanged;
		Map.Clicked += OnMapClick;
		Map.ErrorOccurred += OnMapError;
		Map.Gestured += OnMapGesture;
		Map.CoverageChanged += OnCoverageChanged;

		// The tile source is a device setting (§4.5) and it is scoped, so the settings screen and
		// this map are looking at the same instance. Subscribing means a change made while a map is
		// on screen restyles it rather than waiting for the next navigation - which is what makes
		// the preview on that screen a preview.
		MapSources.Changed += OnMapSourceChanged;
	}

	/// <summary>
	/// The camera is a parameter, so a caller that changes it expects the map to follow -
	/// the alternative is every caller holding an <c>@@ref</c> just to move the view. Only
	/// applies once the base map is attached; before that the new value is picked up by
	/// <see cref="MapOptions"/> at init.
	/// </summary>
	protected override async Task OnParametersSetAsync()
	{
		// Non-null only once InitAsync has succeeded, so this is also the "is the base map
		// attached yet" test - before that the new value is picked up by MapOptions at init.
		if (_appliedCamera is { } applied && Camera != applied)
		{
			_appliedCamera = Camera;
			await Map.SetCameraAsync(Camera);
		}

		// After the camera, never before it: both move the same view, and a caller that changed
		// the pair together means the box.
		await ApplyBoundsAsync();

		// A caller-supplied source changing is the same event as the device's changing, and is
		// handled the same way - see OnMapSourceChanged, which this shares so the two cannot
		// disagree about what a restyle involves.
		await ApplySourceAsync();
	}

	protected override async Task OnAfterRenderAsync(bool firstRender)
	{
		if (_initialised)
		{
			return;
		}

		_initialised = true;

		try
		{
			// Captured before the await, because the parameter can change while the base map is
			// attaching. A caller that only learns where to open - from a device read, say - after
			// its first render would otherwise lose the move entirely: OnParametersSetAsync sees no
			// applied camera yet and returns, and this method would then record the *new* value as
			// applied without ever having sent it, leaving the map on the old one for good.
			// Before the camera is captured, because it is a device read and the answer decides
			// what tiles the map opens with. Idempotent, so a page holding several maps - or one
			// the settings screen has already read for - pays for it once.
			await MapSources.LoadAsync();

			MapCamera requested = Camera;

			MapSource source = Wanted;

			await Map.InitAsync(_host, new MapOptions(requested, ShowUserLocation, AllowRotation, source));
			_appliedCamera = requested;
			_appliedSource = source;

			if (Camera != requested)
			{
				_appliedCamera = Camera;
				await Map.SetCameraAsync(Camera);
			}

			// The first fit, and the one that matters: the box was almost certainly set before the
			// base map existed, so OnParametersSetAsync found nothing attached and returned.
			await ApplyBoundsAsync();

			if (OnReady.HasDelegate)
			{
				await OnReady.InvokeAsync();
			}
		}
		catch (Exception exception)
		{
			// Logged in full as well as shown: the banner has room for a sentence, and the thing
			// that stopped a map coming up - a module that would not import, a style that would
			// not parse - is in the stack rather than in the message.
			DiagnosticLog.WriteError("bringing up the base map", exception);
			_error = DiagnosticLog.Summarise(exception);
			await InvokeAsync(StateHasChanged);
		}
	}

	private void OnViewportChanged(MapViewport viewport)
	{
		_viewport = viewport;
		_ = InvokeAsync(async () =>
		{
			StateHasChanged();
			if (OnViewportReported.HasDelegate)
			{
				await OnViewportReported.InvokeAsync(viewport);
			}
		});
	}

	/// <summary>
	/// Which tiles this map should be drawing: what the caller asked for, or the rider's own
	/// setting when it asked for nothing. See <see cref="Source"/>.
	/// </summary>
	private MapSource Wanted => Source ?? MapSources.Effective;

	/// <summary>
	/// The rider changed which tiles go under their maps. A no-op on a map whose source its caller
	/// is holding - <see cref="Wanted"/> does not move, so <see cref="ApplySourceAsync"/> finds
	/// nothing to do.
	/// </summary>
	private void OnMapSourceChanged() => _ = InvokeAsync(ApplySourceAsync);

	/// <summary>
	/// Brings the base map into line with <see cref="Wanted"/>. Pushed straight at it rather than
	/// re-initialising: <c>SetSourceAsync</c> swaps the style and keeps the camera, so a map being
	/// watched while the setting changes stays on the ground it was showing.
	/// <para>
	/// Silent on failure. A base map that has gone away has already stated its own error, and a
	/// second one over the top of it says nothing the rider can act on.
	/// </para>
	/// </summary>
	private async Task ApplySourceAsync()
	{
		// Null until InitAsync has succeeded - see the field. A map that has not attached picks the
		// current source up at init, and one that failed to attach is already showing why.
		if (_appliedSource is not { } applied || _error is not null)
		{
			return;
		}

		MapSource wanted = Wanted;

		if (wanted == applied)
		{
			return;
		}

		_appliedSource = wanted;

		// A new source deserves a fresh verdict - the last one's complaint says nothing about it.
		// This is also what lets a caller answering OnTileError with a different source hear about
		// the replacement failing too. The retry is reset with it, for the same reason: it is spent
		// against a source, not against the component.
		_tileError = null;
		_retriedSourceTicks = 0;

		// Different tiles, different coverage, and no verdict on the new ones yet. Held to null
		// rather than to true so the banner cannot survive into a source it was never about - the
		// module forgets its own last answer on the same call.
		_coverage = null;

		// The device setting reaches here through MapSourceState's event rather than through a
		// parameter, so nothing re-renders on its own - and both banners cleared above are on
		// screen until something does.
		StateHasChanged();

		try { await Map.SetSourceAsync(wanted); }
		catch (Exception exception)
		{
			// Still not shown - see the remarks - but no longer invisible. A restyle that fails
			// leaves the map on the tiles it already had, which is the right behaviour and looks
			// exactly like a setting that did not take.
			DiagnosticLog.WriteError($"restyling the map to {wanted.Kind}", exception);
		}
	}

	/// <summary>
	/// Frames the map on <see cref="Bounds"/>, if it has been given one it has not already been
	/// framed on. See <see cref="Bounds"/> for why this is not simply a camera.
	/// <para>
	/// Silent on failure, like <see cref="ApplySourceAsync"/>: a map that could not be framed is
	/// still a map showing the right ground at the wrong zoom, and a rider can pinch. A map that
	/// has gone away entirely has already said so above.
	/// </para>
	/// </summary>
	private async Task ApplyBoundsAsync()
	{
		// Null until InitAsync has succeeded - the same "is the base map attached" test the camera
		// uses. A fit needs a canvas to measure, so before that there is nothing to fit against.
		if (_appliedCamera is null || _error is not null)
			return;

		if (Bounds is null)
		{
			// Forgotten rather than kept. A caller that hands over null has stopped talking about a
			// fixed extent - the track page does it while loading the next track - and the same box
			// arriving again after that is a fresh request to frame, not a repeat of this one.
			_appliedBounds = null;
			return;
		}

		if (Bounds is not { } wanted || wanted == _appliedBounds)
			return;

		_appliedBounds = wanted;

		try { await Map.FitBoundsAsync(wanted, BoundsPaddingPx, BoundsMaxZoomLevel); }
		catch (Exception exception)
		{
			DiagnosticLog.WriteError("framing the map on the route", exception);
		}
	}

	/// <summary>
	/// The base map reported something it could not do. Kept only if it is the first - see
	/// <see cref="_tileError"/> - and told to the caller on the same terms.
	/// <para>
	/// <strong>Logged on different terms from the ones it is shown on.</strong> The screen keeps
	/// the first message because a source that cannot be read raises one per tile and a flickering
	/// banner helps nobody. The log wants the opposite: whether a map failed once on a bad glyph or
	/// is failing continuously is most of the diagnosis, and it is invisible on screen. So the
	/// first is written in full and the count is written every so often after it - which is how a
	/// rider's log says "this map never drew a tile" rather than "something went wrong once".
	/// </para>
	/// </summary>
	private void OnMapError(string message)
	{
		_mapErrors++;

		if (_mapErrors == 1)
		{
			DiagnosticLog.Write($"Base map error, drawing {_appliedSource?.Kind.ToString() ?? "nothing yet"}: {message}");
		}
		else if (_mapErrors % MapErrorLogEvery == 0)
		{
			DiagnosticLog.Write($"Base map error #{_mapErrors} (still failing): {message}");
		}

		if (_tileError is not null)
		{
			return;
		}

		_tileError = message;
		_ = InvokeAsync(async () =>
		{
			StateHasChanged();

			if (OnTileError.HasDelegate)
			{
				await OnTileError.InvokeAsync(message);
			}

			await RetryOfflineSourceAsync();
		});
	}

	/// <summary>
	/// One re-resolve of an offline pack after the base map said it could not read it.
	/// <para>
	/// <strong>Why an offline pack alone gets this.</strong> Its archive is served over loopback,
	/// and the URL carries a port the OS assigned and a secret this run generated - so the address
	/// inside the style document is the one thing under a map that can stop being true while the
	/// map is still on screen. A phone that suspends the app long enough for the listener to go
	/// (a long pack download in the background is the way it happens) leaves every tile request
	/// aimed at a port nothing answers, and the rider's map never comes back without a restart.
	/// Asking C# for the source again re-resolves that URL - and starts the server again if it has
	/// stopped - which is a fix the rider does not have to be told about.
	/// </para>
	/// <para>
	/// <strong>Not more than once every <see cref="SourceRetryCooldownMs"/>.</strong> A pack that is
	/// genuinely unreadable would otherwise restyle the map on every failed tile, forever. The
	/// banner is what a failure inside the cooldown gets, because at that point the rider does need
	/// to know.
	/// </para>
	/// </summary>
	private async Task RetryOfflineSourceAsync()
	{
		if (_error is not null || _appliedSource is not { Kind: MapSourceKind.Offline } offline)
		{
			return;
		}

		long now = Environment.TickCount64;

		if (_retriedSourceTicks != 0 && now - _retriedSourceTicks < SourceRetryCooldownMs)
		{
			return;
		}

		_retriedSourceTicks = now;

		DiagnosticLog.Write($"Re-resolving offline pack '{offline.PackId}' after the base map could not read it.");

		try
		{
			await Map.SetSourceAsync(offline);

			// Optimistic, and safe: if the fresh URL fails too the next error puts the banner
			// straight back - this field is what the handler above tests before keeping one.
			_tileError = null;
			StateHasChanged();
		}
		catch (Exception exception)
		{
			DiagnosticLog.WriteError($"re-resolving offline pack '{offline.PackId}'", exception);
		}
	}

	/// <summary>
	/// The base map settled somewhere with different coverage (see
	/// <see cref="IMapInterop.CoverageChanged"/>). Not logged: riding off the edge of a downloaded
	/// region is a thing riders do, not a fault.
	/// </summary>
	private void OnCoverageChanged(MapCoverage coverage)
	{
		_coverage = coverage;

		// Arrives on whichever thread the JS interop callback landed on, like every other one here.
		_ = InvokeAsync(StateHasChanged);
	}

	/// <summary>
	/// Whether to tell the rider the map has nothing under it here. Silent while either error
	/// banner is up: two explanations for one grey screen is worse than either alone.
	/// </summary>
	private bool ShowNoTilesWarning =>
		_coverage is { HasTiles: false } && _error is null && _tileError is null;

	/// <summary>
	/// The zoom in the warning, floored - tiles are requested at the whole number below the zoom
	/// the map draws at, and that is the number a pack states its range in.
	/// </summary>
	private string TileZoomText =>
		Math.Floor(_coverage?.ZoomLevel ?? 0).ToString("0", CultureInfo.InvariantCulture);

	/// <summary>
	/// The rider moved the map with their own hand. Forwarded unfiltered - which gestures matter
	/// is the caller's business, and the only page that has an opinion is the live ride map.
	/// </summary>
	private void OnMapGesture(MapGesture gesture)
	{
		if (!OnGesture.HasDelegate)
		{
			return;
		}
		// Arrives on whichever thread the JS interop callback landed on; the EventCallback has to
		// be raised on the renderer's. Same reason as OnMapClick below.
		_ = InvokeAsync(() => OnGesture.InvokeAsync(gesture));
	}

	private void OnMapClick(MapClick click)
	{
		if (!OnMapClicked.HasDelegate)
		{
			return;
		}
		// The event arrives on whatever thread the JS interop callback lands on; the
		// EventCallback has to be raised on the renderer's.
		_ = InvokeAsync(() => OnMapClicked.InvokeAsync(click));
	}

	/// <summary>
	/// Move the base map's camera. The overlay re-registers via the next viewport event.
	/// <para>
	/// <paramref name="animation"/> is passed straight through - see
	/// <see cref="IMapInterop.SetCameraAsync"/> for when a camera should glide rather than jump.
	/// Zero, the default, is the jump every caller had before the option existed.
	/// </para>
	/// </summary>
	/// <param name="camera">Where the map should be looking.</param>
	/// <param name="animation">How long to take getting there; zero puts it there at once.</param>
	public ValueTask SetCameraAsync(MapCamera camera, TimeSpan animation = default) =>
		Map.SetCameraAsync(camera, animation);

	public async ValueTask DisposeAsync()
	{
		Map.ViewportChanged -= OnViewportChanged;
		Map.Clicked -= OnMapClick;
		Map.ErrorOccurred -= OnMapError;
		Map.Gestured -= OnMapGesture;
		Map.CoverageChanged -= OnCoverageChanged;
		MapSources.Changed -= OnMapSourceChanged;
		if (_initialised)
		{
			await Map.DisposeAsync();
		}
	}
}

<style>
	.dlr-map-container {
		position: relative;
		width: 100%;
		min-height: 480px;

		/*
		  Clips the overlay to the map. The overlay canvas is sized from the viewport the base
		  map reported, and a viewport that arrives a frame stale - mid-resize, or on the first
		  layout pass on a phone - describes a canvas larger than the container currently is.
		  Without this the difference paints over whatever is below the map, which is how a
		  track ended up drawn past the edge of the map window on device.
		*/
		overflow: hidden;
	}
	.dlr-map-base {
		position: absolute;
		inset: 0;
		width: 100%;
		height: 100%;
		background: #eef1f520;
	}
	/*
	  The compass, top left, only once the map is off north (§4.5). map.maplibre.js adds
	  MapLibre's NavigationControl and toggles `dlr-map-rotated` on the base-map element as the
	  bearing crosses half a degree; this is the half that decides what that means on screen.

	  Hidden with visibility rather than display:none, because MapLibre measures its control
	  containers and a control in a zero-size box reports a zero-size box back.
	*/
	.dlr-map-base:not(.dlr-map-rotated) .maplibregl-ctrl-top-left {
		visibility: hidden;
	}

	/*
	  Sized and placed to match the live ride's hamburger on the opposite edge - same tap
	  target, same distance down the screen - from the shared tokens in app.css.

	  The inset is the part that was actually wrong: MapLibre's default is a flat 10 px from
	  the top of its container, which ignores the safe area and tucks the compass under the
	  notch on a phone. `--dlr-map-control-top` is the same expression the hamburger uses, so
	  the two cannot drift apart.

	  The left inset is the plain token and not a second `env(safe-area-inset-left)`: every
	  container this map sits in already starts to the right of the nav rail, and the rail is
	  what carries the landscape notch (MainLayout.razor.css). Adding the inset again here
	  would push the compass a notch's width into the map for nothing.

	  The z-index lifts it within the base map. The overlay canvas still paints above it, but
	  the overlay is transparent everywhere it has not drawn, so the compass shows through and
	  stays clickable - the overlay is pointer-events: none.
	*/
	.dlr-map-base .maplibregl-ctrl-top-left {
		top: var(--dlr-map-control-top);
		left: var(--dlr-map-control-inset);
		z-index: 3;
	}

	.dlr-map-base .maplibregl-ctrl-top-left .maplibregl-ctrl-group {
		/* MapLibre's own 10 px margin, now that the container carries the inset. */
		margin: 0;
		border-radius: 8px;
		box-shadow: 0 2px 8px rgba(0,0,0,0.35);
	}

	.dlr-map-base .maplibregl-ctrl-top-left .maplibregl-ctrl-compass {
		width: var(--dlr-map-control-size);
		height: var(--dlr-map-control-size);
		border-radius: 8px;
	}

	/*
	  The needle is a background image on the button's inner span, and MapLibre rotates that
	  span to point north. Enlarging the button alone would leave a small needle in a big box,
	  so the image scales with it - background-size rather than a transform, which would fight
	  the rotation.
	*/
	.dlr-map-base .maplibregl-ctrl-top-left .maplibregl-ctrl-compass .maplibregl-ctrl-icon {
		background-size: 28px 28px;
	}

	/* A map wired for picking says so with the pointer - the provider's own canvas sets
	   its cursor, so this has to reach inside it. */
	.dlr-map-container.picking .dlr-map-base,
	.dlr-map-container.picking .dlr-map-base * {
		cursor: crosshair;
	}
	.dlr-map-error {
		margin-top: 0.5rem;
		padding: 0.75rem 1rem;
		background: #fff3cd40;
		border: 1px solid #ffe69c;
		border-radius: 4px;
	}
	.dlr-map-error strong { display: block; margin-bottom: 0.25rem; }
	.dlr-map-error p { margin: 0; }
	.dlr-map-error-detail { margin-top: 0.35rem; opacity: 0.75; font-size: 0.8rem; overflow-wrap: anywhere; }

	/* The two banners that sit over the base map rather than under it: the map still works, and
	   the point is that what is *missing* from it is not obvious by looking. One look, two
	   placements - the palette lived in three near-identical copies before. */
	.dlr-map-banner {
		position: absolute;
		z-index: 2;
		padding: 0.6rem 0.85rem;
		background: #fff3cd;
		color: #4a3c00;
		border: 1px solid #ffe69c;
		border-radius: 4px;
		font-size: 0.85rem;
	}
	.dlr-map-banner strong { display: block; margin-bottom: 0.2rem; }
	.dlr-map-banner p { margin: 0; }

	.dlr-map-overlay-error {
		left: 0.5rem;
		right: 0.5rem;
		bottom: 0.5rem;
	}
	.dlr-map-overlay-error-detail { margin-top: 0.35rem; opacity: 0.75; font-size: 0.78rem; }

	/* At the top and centred, which is what keeps it off the two top corners - the compass owns
	   one and the live ride's hamburger owns the other, and a message that covers a control is a
	   message that eats a tap. `pointer-events: none` because the map under it is what the rider
	   is trying to use. */
	.dlr-map-no-tiles {
		top: var(--dlr-map-control-top);
		left: 50%;
		transform: translateX(-50%);
		width: max-content;
		max-width: min(24rem, calc(100% - 2rem - 2 * var(--dlr-map-control-size)));
		padding: 0.5rem 0.75rem;
		text-align: center;
		pointer-events: none;
	}
	.dlr-map-no-tiles p { font-size: 0.78rem; opacity: 0.85; }
</style>
